{=============================================================================
  Helium balloon buoyancy and acceleration (exercise)
  CoolSolve Library model CSL-0241
 -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
  A spherical helium balloon of diameter 10 m. The air density is given,
  and the helium density is one seventh of it. The carried mass is written
  as 70*2. The buoyancy force is the weight of the displaced air, the total
  mass adds the helium, and the acceleration follows Newton's second law
  as written. Teaching exercise Cengel 1-101 from the KU Leuven collection.
  The equation window names nobody. g = 9.81 stays; g = g# is a comment.
  No fluid call and no cycle: no diagram-state arrays.

  Inputs : rho_air, d, m, g
  Outputs: a, F_b, m_tot
  Units  : SI - temperatures in °C, pressures in Pa, energies in J
  Source : Cengel 1-101, file 01-101.EES; authors S. Quoilin (inventory)
           - see README.md
  Licence: MIT. Status: verified against the EES stored solution.
=============================================================================}

"!Data"
rho_air = 1.16                                           "air density [kg/m^3], as written"
d = 10                                                   "balloon diameter [m], as written"
m = 70 * 2                                               "carried mass [kg], 70*2 as written"
g = 9.81                                                 "gravitational acceleration [m/s^2], as written"
"or, as in the original:"
"g = g#"                                                 "left as a comment; g# is not used"
rho_he = rho_air/7                                       "helium density [kg/m^3], as written"

"!Buoyancy and acceleration"
F_b = rho_air * g * V_balloon                            "buoyancy force [N], as written"
V_balloon = 4/3 * pi * r^3                               "balloon volume [m^3]; the window writes pi"
r = d/2                                                  "balloon radius [m], as written"
m_tot = m + rho_he * V_balloon                           "total mass [kg], carried mass plus helium, as written"
F_b - m_tot*g = m_tot * a                                "upward acceleration [m/s^2], as written"
